## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical OTCA access control design with residency, privacy tagging, policy-as-code, and immutable auditing. It expects concrete choices and testing plans rather than abstract principles.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant residency and data tagging
- Policy-as-code with OPA
- Short-lived token-based authentication
- Immutable auditing sinks (S3 Object Lock/OpenSearch)
- Dynamic policy updates and backpressure testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo authorization check (conceptual)
function authorize(user, query){
  if (!user || user.tenant !== query.tenant) throw new Error('Denied');
  if (!policy.enables(user, query)) throw new Error('Denied');
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test policy updates for zero-downtime?
- How would you model data-tags and ensure privacy classifications propagate?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design resilient, scalable OTCA pipelines that cope with edge intermittency and cross-region data flight. It emphasizes practical consistency, privacy, and schema strategy under real-world constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Edge buffering and bounded memory
- Per-tenant privacy masking
- Deduplication and idempotent writes
- Avro schema evolution (backward/forward compat)
- Region-aware routing and cross-region aggregation
- Backpressure and QoS controls

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo dedupe check (edge/local)
function isDuplicate(event, seen) {
  const key = `${event.tenant_id}:${event.event_id}`;
  const last = seen.get(key) || -Infinity;
  if (event.ts <= last) return true;
  seen.set(key, event.ts);
  return false;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate cross-region reconciliation latency?
- What changes if edge buffers exceed memory under bursty traffic?

